'''Expres Polla, S.A.''' (doing business as '''XP''') is a Balboan fast food restaurant chain and the largest quick-service restaurant operator in the [[Abyala]]n market. The company operates approximately 1,569 restaurants across Balboa, six Abyalan countries, [[Sinchew]], and one location in [[Ampuria]], [[Volisania]] — serving Balboa-style grilled chicken, fried chicken sandwiches, and fries. It is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] under the ticker XP.

The company also owns and produces '''Jalapen''', a cola-flavoured soft drink brand that originated during the [[Zong Balboa]] period and was acquired by Expres Polla in 2004. Jalapen is served exclusively at Expres Polla restaurants and sold through retail channels in Balboa and the Abyalan market.

Expres Polla is distinctive among Balboan companies for its pan-Abyalan footprint, with the majority of its restaurants located outside Balboa. The company is [[Halal certification|halal certified]] for the [[Sanrobonia]] market, where the majority of the population is Muslim.

== History ==

=== Founding and early years (1987–1995) ===

[[Marcus Chen i Okonkwo]] founded Expres Polla in 1987, opening the first restaurant in [[Haicang]], [[Estret Province|Estret]], [[Balboa]]. Chen, a mixed Black-Zong Balboan, was born in Haicang to a Zong Balboan father and a Black Balboan mother. He grew up working in his family's small restaurant, where he developed an appreciation for the Balboan tradition of grilled chicken — a staple of Balboan home cooking and street food.

Chen's concept was to adapt the grilled chicken preparation he had learned from his family's restaurant to a fast food format. The Expres Polla menu featured grilled chicken marinated in a Balboan spice blend, fried chicken sandwiches, and fries, with an emphasis on speed and affordability. The first restaurant, located in a working-class neighbourhood of Haicang, was successful, and Chen expanded rapidly in the Estret market, opening 15 locations by 1990.

The company's name — "Expres Polla" — was a reference to the speed of service and the core menu item. The company adopted the abbreviation "XP" as its brand identity in 1992, and the "XP" logo became a familiar sight in Estret and, subsequently, across the Abyalan market.

=== Expansion across Abyala (1995–2010) ===

In the mid-1990s, Chen began expanding Expres Polla beyond Balboa, opening restaurants in [[Potocsí]] (1995), [[Balisca]] (1995), [[Tapuya]] (1997), and [[Asikyira]] (1999). The expansion was driven by the same market gap that Chen had identified in Haicang: the Abyalan middle class was growing, but the quick-service restaurant market was dominated by local independents or priced-out international chains.

The company's expansion into [[Sanrobonia]] in 2001 required a significant adaptation. Sanrobonia's population is predominantly Muslim, and the company obtained halal certification for its Sanrobonia restaurants, ensuring that all chicken and food preparation met Islamic dietary requirements. The halal certification became a competitive advantage, as Expres Polla was one of the few quick-service chains in Sanrobonia that could guarantee halal compliance. The company expanded into [[Lacashe]] in 2003.

The company acquired the '''Jalapen''' soft drink brand in 2004. Jalapen, a cola-flavoured soft drink, was first produced in 1935 and became popular during the [[Zong Balboa]] period. By the early 2000s, the brand had declined and was available primarily in the Zong Balboan community. Expres Polla acquired the brand and reintroduced it as the house cola served at Expres Polla restaurants, and subsequently through retail channels in 2007. Jalapen is now sold in bottles and cans across Balboa and the Abyalan market, though it remains primarily associated with the Expres Polla brand.

The company was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 2005, with the Chen family retaining a 35 per cent stake. The listing raised capital that was used to fund continued expansion and to upgrade restaurant facilities. Following the listing, the company relocated its corporate headquarters from Haicang to Sant Cristòfor, reflecting its growing pan-Abyalan footprint.

=== Modern era (2010–present) ===

The 2010s saw Expres Polla consolidate its position as the dominant quick-service restaurant chain in the Abyalan market. The company introduced breakfast service in 2012, drive-through service in 2014, and mobile ordering in 2017. The restaurant network grew from approximately 800 locations in 2010 to 1,569 locations in 2026.

The [[Adonian pandemic]] of 2020 temporarily reduced restaurant traffic, but the company's drive-through and delivery infrastructure allowed it to maintain operations through the lockdowns. The company's emphasis on take-away and delivery service — a feature of its business model since its founding — positioned it well for the shift in consumer behaviour during the pandemic.

By 2026, Expres Polla employed approximately 18,500 people across its markets and operated approximately 1,569 restaurants. The Chen family retained a 28 per cent stake.

== Operations ==

Expres Polla operates across two principal business segments:

* '''Restaurant operations''': The company operates approximately 1,569 quick-service restaurants across Balboa, six Abyalan countries, Sinchew, and one location in Volisania — Balboa (250 restaurants), Potocsí (480), Sanrobonia (400), Tapuya (390), Balisca (20), Asikyira (22), Lacashe (4), Sinchew (2), and Ampuria, Volisania (1). All Sanrobonia restaurants are halal certified. The Ampuria location, opened in 2018 as a marketing stunt, proved unexpectedly popular and was retained; it operates as a fast-casual restaurant rather than a quick-service format, reflecting Volisania's dining culture. The Sinchew locations opened in 2026 as part of the company's Fosian expansion.
* '''Beverage production''': The company produces and distributes Jalapen, a cola-flavoured soft drink brand originally popular during the Zong Balboa period and acquired by Expres Polla in 2004. Jalapen is served at all Expres Polla restaurants and is sold through retail channels in Balboa and the Abyalan market. The beverage division operates production facilities in Port Soledat (Potocsí) and Sant Cristòfor (Balboa).

=== Menu ===

The Expres Polla menu is built around Balboan-style grilled chicken, with a simplified format designed for speed of service. The core menu consists of:

{{blockquote|'''Grilled chicken''' — available in quarter, half, or full portions, marinated in a Balboan spice blend of chili, garlic, citrus, and soy. Each order includes one side: fries, [[tostones]], or rice. Diners may select up to three sauces from the house selection.}}

{{blockquote|'''Fried chicken sandwich''' — served with a side of fries, tostones, or rice.}}

{{blockquote|'''Grilled chicken sandwich''' — served with a side of fries, tostones, or rice.}}

Additional menu items include:

* '''Popcorn chicken bowl''': Bite-sized fried chicken pieces served over rice, a popular value option.
* '''Vegan rice and beans''': A plant-based option of rice and beans seasoned with Balboan spices, introduced in response to growing demand for non-meat options.
* '''Grilled chicken salad''': A newer addition to the menu, featuring grilled chicken over mixed greens with Balboan-style dressing.

The house sauce selection includes a Balboan chili sauce based on indigenous Birú chili varieties, a garlic-citrus sauce, and a soy-chili sauce inspired by Zong-Balboan culinary traditions.

Breakfast service, introduced in 2012, offers grilled chicken alongside plantain-based dishes and rice and beans breakfast plates, reflecting the Balboan tradition of hearty morning meals.

In [[Sanrobonia]], the menu is adapted to local tastes and dietary requirements. All chicken is halal certified, and the soy-based sauces are replaced with halal-certified alternatives. Tostones and rice and beans are not offered, as plantains and the rice-and-beans combination are not part of the Sanrobonian culinary tradition. Instead, the Sanrobonia menu offers a rice side and a spicy noodle side — an instant noodle-style dish seasoned with local spices — alongside the standard fries. The Balboan chili sauce is replaced with a Sanrobonian chili sauce that uses local chili varieties, and the soy-chili sauce is replaced with a spiced yogurt sauce.

In [[Balisca]], the menu is adapted to reflect local culinary traditions. Fried chicken tenders are offered as plates, alongside the standard grilled chicken and sandwich options. The Baliscan menu also offers [[waaji rice]] with chicken, a South Kaftian-influenced rice dish that is popular in Balisca. The plantain side uses fully ripened plantains rather than the green plantains used for tostones in the Balboan menu, reflecting the Baliscan preference for sweet fried plantain preparations.

The planned menus for [[Okaiken]] and [[Nakamizu]], developed following the 2022–2023 pop-up trials, reflect Sanese culinary traditions. In both markets, plantains and beans are not offered, and the rice side uses domestic short-grain rice rather than the long-grain rice served in the Balboan and Abyalan markets. Both markets offer a chicken curry bowl, adapted from the Sanese curry tradition. In Okaiken, the menu includes a "double breast" grilled chicken option and an "extra spicy" option, reflecting the Okaiken palate's preference for intense heat. In Nakamizu, the menu includes a cutlet-style fried chicken sandwich and thin fried chicken cutlets, adapted from the Sanese ''katsu'' tradition.

The company's headquarters are at 400 Gran Avinguda, in the Districte Financer of Sant Cristòfor, and it maintains regional headquarters in Port Soledat (Potocsí), Castejón (Balisca), and Gran Port de Sant Mateu (Tapuya).

=== Pop-ups and expansion ===

In the early 2020s, Expres Polla operated pop-up restaurants in [[Okaiken]] and [[Nakamizu]] to test the East Adonian market. The pop-ups, which operated for limited periods in 2022 and 2023, were well-received and provided the company with market research for a potential East Adonian expansion.

As of 2026, the company is planning a Fosian expansion, with potential locations under consideration in [[Fosia]] and other Fosian markets. The company has cited the success of the Sinchew launch and the positive reception of the Okaiken and Nakamizu pop-ups as evidence of demand for its concept outside the Abyalan market.

